// Gobra contracts for ID generation — F8.
//
// Each call to Next() returns a value strictly greater than the previous,
// starting at 1. This guarantees global uniqueness and per-author
// monotonicity (any subset of issued IDs is also strictly monotonic in
// issue order).
//
// Stream 3 Phase 2a: introduce the LockP() permission predicate over the
// Generator. Conceptually: "the mutex `mu` is initialized and protects the
// `next` field." The actual mutex permission discipline is still bypassed
// by `// @ trusted` on Next()/Peek()/Current()/SetCurrent() — that
// discharge happens in Phase 2b. What this file does today is set up the
// *shape* of the proof: callers can hold `acc(g.LockP(), _)` as a
// permission token, and the constructors below in ids.go fold this
// predicate after construction so it is available. Mirrors S3P1a on
// `internal/clock`.

package ids

// LockP is the lock-invariant predicate for *Generator. It abstracts the
// resource the mutex `mu` protects: write permission to `next`. Once Phase
// 2b drops the `// @ trusted` markers on Next()/Peek()/Current()/
// SetCurrent(), those methods will `unfold`/`fold` LockP() under the lock
// to access `next` legally.
//
// Body deliberately minimal: only `acc(&g.next)`. The mutex itself
// (`g.mu`) is a value-typed `sync.Mutex` field, and our `stubs/sync`
// declares no permission tokens on `Mutex` — so there is nothing to
// assert about `g.mu` here beyond the field-existence implied by `acc(g)`
// at the call sites. This keeps Phase 2a a pure additive setup with no
// stub surgery.
pred (g *Generator) LockP() {
	acc(&g.next) && g.next >= 1
}

// @ requires acc(g)
// @ ensures acc(g) && result == old(g.next) && g.next == old(g.next) + 1
// @ ensures result >= 1
// @ func (g *Generator) GhostNext() (result int64)

// @ requires acc(g, 1/2)
// @ ensures acc(g, 1/2) && result == g.next && g.next == old(g.next)
// @ pure
// @ func (g *Generator) GhostPeek() (result int64)
